## ud8.5, continued
ud8.5:17.2 | S: Before the group of mendicants, in the middle, <j>the Buddha, | T: Honored, surrounded, in the midst of the group of monks, the Blessed One,
ud8.5:17.3 | S: the Teacher who rolled forth <j>the present dispensation, | T: Teacher, proceeding here in the Dhamma,
ud8.5:17.4 | S: the great seer went to the mango grove. | T: the great seer, went to the mango grove.
ud8.5:17.5 | S: He addressed the mendicant named Cundaka: | T: He addressed the monk named Cundaka,
ud8.5:17.6 | S: “Spread out my folded robe so I can lie down.” | T: “Spread it out, folded in four for me to lie down.”
ud8.5:18.1 | S: The evolved one urged Cunda, | T: Ordered by the One of developed mind,
ud8.5:18.2 | S: who quickly spread the folded robe. | T: Cundaka quickly set it out, folded in four.
ud8.5:18.3 | S: The Teacher lay down so tired, | T: The Teacher lay down, seeming very tired,
ud8.5:18.4 | S: while Cunda sat there before him. | T: and Cundaka sat down there before him.
ud8.5:19.1 | S: Then the Buddha said to Venerable Ānanda, | T: Then the Blessed One addressed Ven. Ānanda,
ud8.5:19.2 | S: “Now it may happen, Ānanda, that others may give rise to some regret for Cunda the smith: | T: “Ānanda, if anyone tries to incite remorse in Cunda the silversmith, saying,
ud8.5:19.3 | S: ‘It’s your loss, respectable Cunda, it’s your misfortune, in that the Realized One was fully quenched after eating his last almsmeal from you.’ | T: ‘It’s no gain for you, friend Cunda, it’s ill-done by you, that the Tathāgata, having eaten your last alms, was totally unbound,’
ud8.5:19.4 | S: You should dispel remorse in Cunda the smith like this: | T: then Cunda’s remorse should be allayed (in this way):
ud8.5:20.1 | S: ‘You’re fortunate, respectable Cunda, you’re so very fortunate, | T: ‘It’s a gain for you, friend Cunda, it’s well-done by you,
ud8.5:20.2 | S: in that the Realized One was fully quenched after eating his last almsmeal from you. | T: that the Tathāgata, having eaten your last alms, was totally unbound.
ud8.5:20.3 | S: I have heard and learned this in the presence of the Buddha. | T: Face to face with the Blessed One have I heard it, face to face have I learned it,
ud8.5:20.4 | S: There are two almsmeal offerings that have identical fruit and result, and are more fruitful and beneficial than other almsmeal offerings. | T: “These two alms are equal to each other in fruit, equal to each other in result, of much greater fruit & reward than any other alms.
ud8.5:20.5 | S: What two? | T: Which two?
ud8.5:20.6 | S: The almsmeal after eating which a Realized One awakens to the supreme perfect awakening; and the almsmeal after eating which he becomes fully extinguished in the element of extinguishment with no residue. | T: The alms that, after having eaten it, the Tathāgata awakens to the unexcelled right self-awakening. And the alms that, after having eaten it, the Tathāgata is unbound by means of the unbinding property with no fuel remaining.
ud8.5:20.7 | S: These two almsmeal offerings have identical fruit and result, and are more fruitful and beneficial than other almsmeal offerings. | T: These are the two alms that are equal to each other in fruit, equal to each other in result, of much greater fruit & reward than any other alms.
ud8.5:21.1 | S: You’ve accumulated a deed that leads to long life, beauty, happiness, glory, heaven, and sovereignty.’ | T: Venerable Cunda the silversmith has accumulated kamma that leads to long life. Venerable Cunda the silversmith has accumulated kamma that leads to beauty. Venerable Cunda the silversmith has accumulated kamma that leads to happiness. Venerable Cunda the silversmith has accumulated kamma that leads to heaven. Venerable Cunda the silversmith has accumulated kamma that leads to rank. Venerable Cunda the silversmith has accumulated kamma that leads to sovereignty.”’
ud8.5:21.2 | S: You should dispel remorse in Cunda the smith like this.” | T: In this way, Ānanda, Cunda the silversmith’s remorse should be allayed.”
ud8.5:22.1 | S: Then, understanding this matter, on that occasion the Buddha expressed this heartfelt sentiment: | T: Then, on realizing the significance of that, the Blessed One on that occasion exclaimed:
ud8.5:23.1 | S: “A giver’s merit grows; | T: For a person giving, merit increases.
ud8.5:23.2 | S: enmity doesn’t build up when you have self-control. | T: For one self-restraining, no animosity is amassed.
ud8.5:23.3 | S: A skillful person gives up bad things— | T: One who is skillful leaves evil behind
ud8.5:23.4 | S: with the end of greed, hate, and delusion, <j>they’re quenched.” | T: and –from the ending of passion, aversion, delusion– is totally unbound.
ud8.5:23.5 | P: Pañcamaṁ. | T: (none)

