## an10.29
an10.29:1.1 | S: “As far as Kāsi and Kosala extend, and as far as the dominion of King Pasenadi of Kosala extends, King Pasenadi is said to be the foremost. | T: [1] “Monks, as far as Kāsi & Kosala extend, as far as the rule of King Pasenadi the Kosalan extends, King Pasenadi the Kosalan is reckoned supreme.
an10.29:1.2 | S: But even King Pasenadi decays and perishes. | T: Yet even in King Pasenadi the Kosalan there is still aberration, there is change.
an10.29:1.3 | S: Seeing this, a learned noble disciple grows disillusioned with it. | T: Seeing this, the instructed disciple of the noble ones grows disenchanted with that.
an10.29:1.4 | S: Their desire fades away even for the foremost, let alone the inferior. | T: Being disenchanted with that, he becomes dispassionate toward what is supreme, and even more so toward what is inferior.
an10.29:2.1 | S: A galaxy extends a thousand times as far as the moon and sun revolve and the dazzling ones light up the quarters. | T: [2] “As far as the sun & moon revolve, illumining the directions with their light, there extends the thousand-fold cosmos.
an10.29:2.2 | S: In that galaxy there are a thousand moons, a thousand suns, a thousand Sinerus king of mountains, a thousand Black Plum Tree Lands, a thousand lands west of Godāvarī, a thousand lands north of Kuru, a thousand lands east of Videha, four thousand oceans, four thousand great kings, a thousand realms of the gods of the four great kings, a thousand realms of the gods of the thirty-three, of the gods of Yama, of the joyful gods, of the gods who love to create, of the gods who control what is created by others, and a thousand realms of divinity. | T: In that thousand-fold cosmos there are a thousand moons, a thousand suns, a thousand Sunerus—kings of mountains; a thousand Rose-apple continents, a thousand Deathless Ox-cart (continents), a thousand northern Kuru (continents), a thousand eastern Videha (continents); four thousand great oceans, four thousand Great Kings, a thousand (heavens of the) Four Great Kings, a thousand (heavens of the) Thirty-three, a thousand (heavens of the) Hours, a thousand (heavens of the) Contented, a thousand heavens of the Devas Delighting in Creation, a thousand heavens of the Devas Wielding Power over the Creations of Others, and a thousand Brahmā worlds.
an10.29:2.3 | S: As far as the galaxy extends, the Great Divinity is said to be the foremost. | T: And in that thousand-fold cosmos, the Great Brahmā is reckoned supreme.
an10.29:2.4 | S: But even the Great Divinity decays and perishes. | T: Yet even in the Great Brahmā there is still aberration, there is change.
an10.29:2.5 | S: Seeing this, a learned noble disciple grows disillusioned with it. | T: Seeing this, the instructed disciple of the noble ones grows disenchanted with that.
an10.29:2.6 | S: Their desire fades away even for the foremost, let alone the inferior. | T: Being disenchanted with that, he becomes dispassionate toward what is supreme, and even more so toward what is inferior.
an10.29:3.1 | S: There comes a time when this cosmos contracts. | T: [3] “There comes a time when this cosmos devolves.
an10.29:3.2 | S: As it contracts, most sentient beings migrate to the realm of sublime luminosity. | T: When the cosmos is devolving, most beings head to (the heaven of) the Radiant.
an10.29:3.3 | S: There they are mind-made, feeding on rapture, self-radiant, wandering in midair, steadily glorious, and they remain like that for a very long time. | T: There they remain for a long, long time—mind-made, feeding on rapture, self-radiant, faring through the sky, abiding in splendor.
an10.29:3.4 | S: When the cosmos is contracting, the gods of sublime luminosity are said to be the foremost. | T: When the cosmos is devolving, the Radiant Devas are reckoned supreme.
an10.29:3.5 | S: But even the gods of sublime luminosity decay and perish. | T: Yet even in the Radiant Devas, there is still aberration, there is change.
an10.29:3.6 | S: Seeing this, a learned noble disciple grows disillusioned with it. | T: Seeing this, the instructed disciple of the noble ones grows disenchanted with that.
an10.29:3.7 | S: Their desire fades away even for the foremost, let alone the inferior. | T: Being disenchanted with that, he becomes dispassionate toward what is supreme, and even more so toward what is inferior.
an10.29:4.1 | S: There are these ten universal dimensions of meditation. | T: [4] “There are these ten totality-dimensions.
an10.29:4.2 | S: What ten? | T: Which ten?
an10.29:4.3 | S: Someone perceives the meditation on universal earth above, below, across, undivided and limitless. | T: One perceives the earth-totality above, below, all-around: non-dual [advayaṁ], immeasurable.
an10.29:4.4 | S: They perceive the meditation on universal water … | T: One perceives the water-totality…
an10.29:4.5 | S: the meditation on universal fire … | T: the fire-totality…
an10.29:4.6 | S: the meditation on universal air … | T: the wind-totality…
an10.29:4.7 | S: the meditation on universal blue … | T: the blue-totality…
an10.29:4.8 | S: the meditation on universal yellow … | T: the yellow-totality…
an10.29:4.9 | S: the meditation on universal red … | T: the red-totality…
an10.29:4.10 | S: the meditation on universal white … | T: the white-totality…
an10.29:4.11 | S: the meditation on universal space … | T: the space-totality…
an10.29:4.12 | S: They perceive the meditation on universal consciousness above, below, across, undivided and limitless. | T: the consciousness-totality above, below, all-around: non-dual, immeasurable.
an10.29:4.13 | S: These are the ten universal dimensions of meditation. | T: These are the ten totality-dimensions.
an10.29:5.1 | S: The best of these ten universal dimensions of meditation is when someone perceives the meditation on universal consciousness above, below, across, undivided and limitless. | T: Now, of these ten totality-dimensions, this is supreme: when one perceives the consciousness-totality above, below, all-around: non-dual, immeasurable.
an10.29:5.2 | S: Some sentient beings perceive like this. | T: And there are beings who are percipient in this way.
an10.29:5.3 | S: But even the sentient beings who perceive like this decay and perish. | T: Yet even in the beings who are percipient in this way there is still aberration, there is change.
an10.29:5.4 | S: Seeing this, a learned noble disciple grows disillusioned with it. | T: Seeing this, the instructed disciple of the noble ones grows disenchanted with that.
an10.29:5.5 | S: Their desire fades away even for the foremost, let alone the inferior. | T: Being disenchanted with that, he becomes dispassionate toward what is supreme, and even more so toward what is inferior.
an10.29:6.1 | S: There are these eight dimensions of mastery. | T: [5] “There are these eight dimensions of (mental) mastery.
an10.29:6.2 | S: What eight? | T: Which eight?
an10.29:6.3 | S: Perceiving form internally, someone sees forms externally, limited, both pretty and ugly. | T: (a) “One percipient of form internally sees forms externally as limited, beautiful & ugly.
an10.29:6.4 | S: Mastering them, they perceive: ‘I know and see.’ | T: Mastering them, he is percipient of ‘I know; I see.’
an10.29:6.5 | S: This is the first dimension of mastery. | T: This is the first dimension of (mental) mastery.
an10.29:7.1 | S: Perceiving form internally, someone sees forms externally, limitless, both pretty and ugly. | T: (b) “One percipient of form internally sees forms externally as immeasurable, beautiful & ugly.
an10.29:7.2 | S: Mastering them, they perceive: ‘I know and see.’ | T: Mastering them, he is percipient of ‘I know; I see.’
an10.29:7.3 | S: This is the second dimension of mastery. | T: This is the second dimension of (mental) mastery.
an10.29:8.1 | S: Not perceiving form internally, someone sees forms externally, limited, both pretty and ugly. | T: (c) “One percipient of the formless internally sees forms externally as limited, beautiful & ugly.
an10.29:8.2 | S: Mastering them, they perceive: ‘I know and see.’ | T: Mastering them, he is percipient of ‘I know; I see.’
an10.29:8.3 | S: This is the third dimension of mastery. | T: This is the third dimension of (mental) mastery.
an10.29:9.1 | S: Not perceiving form internally, someone sees forms externally, limitless, both pretty and ugly. | T: (d) “One percipient of the formless internally sees forms externally as immeasurable, beautiful & ugly.
an10.29:9.2 | S: Mastering them, they perceive: ‘I know and see.’ | T: Mastering them, he is percipient of ‘I know; I see.’
an10.29:9.3 | S: This is the fourth dimension of mastery. | T: This is the fourth dimension of (mental) mastery.
an10.29:10.1 | S: Not perceiving form internally, someone sees forms externally, blue, with blue color and blue appearance. | T: (e) “One percipient of the formless internally sees forms externally as blue, blue in their color, blue in their features, blue in their glow.
an10.29:10.2 | S: They’re like a flax flower that’s blue, with blue color and blue appearance. Or a cloth from Varanasi that’s smoothed on both sides, blue, with blue color and blue appearance. | T: Just as a flax-flower is blue, blue in its color, blue in its features, blue in its glow, or just as Vārāṇasī muslin, smooth on both sides, is blue, blue in its color, blue in its features, blue in its glow,
an10.29:10.3 | S: In the same way, not perceiving form internally, someone sees forms externally, blue, with blue color and blue appearance. | T: in the same way one percipient of the formless internally sees forms externally as blue, blue in their color, blue in their features, blue in their glow.
an10.29:10.4 | S: Mastering them, they perceive: ‘I know and see.’ | T: Mastering them, he is percipient of ‘I know; I see.’
an10.29:10.5 | S: This is the fifth dimension of mastery. | T: This is the fifth dimension of (mental) mastery.
an10.29:11.1 | S: Not perceiving form internally, someone sees forms externally, yellow, with yellow color and yellow appearance. | T: (f) “One percipient of the formless internally sees forms externally as yellow, yellow in their color, yellow in their features, yellow in their glow.
an10.29:11.2 | S: They’re like a champak flower that’s yellow, with yellow color and yellow appearance. Or a cloth from Varanasi that’s smoothed on both sides, yellow, with yellow color and yellow appearance. | T: Just as a kaṇṇikāra-flower is yellow, yellow in its color, yellow in its features, yellow in its glow, or just as Vārāṇasī muslin, smooth on both sides, is yellow, yellow in its color, yellow in its features, yellow in its glow,
an10.29:11.3 | S: In the same way, not perceiving form internally, someone sees forms externally, yellow, with yellow color and yellow appearance. | T: in the same way one percipient of the formless internally sees forms externally as yellow, yellow in their color, yellow in their features, yellow in their glow.
an10.29:11.4 | S: Mastering them, they perceive: ‘I know and see.’ | T: Mastering them, he is percipient of ‘I know; I see.’
an10.29:11.5 | S: This is the sixth dimension of mastery. | T: This is the sixth dimension of (mental) mastery.
an10.29:12.1 | S: Not perceiving form internally, someone sees forms externally, red, with red color and red appearance. | T: (g) “One percipient of the formless internally sees forms externally as red, red in their color, red in their features, red in their glow.
an10.29:12.2 | S: They’re like a scarlet mallow flower that’s red, with red color and red appearance. Or a cloth from Varanasi that’s smoothed on both sides, red, with red color and red appearance. | T: Just as a bandha-jīvaka-flower is red, red in its color, red in its features, red in its glow, or just as Vārāṇasī muslin, smooth on both sides, is red, red in its color, red in its features, red in its glow,
an10.29:12.3 | S: In the same way, not perceiving form internally, someone sees forms externally, red, with red color and red appearance. | T: in the same way one percipient of the formless internally sees forms externally as red, red in their color, red in their features, red in their glow.
an10.29:12.4 | S: Mastering them, they perceive: ‘I know and see.’ | T: Mastering them, he is percipient of ‘I know; I see.’
an10.29:12.5 | S: This is the seventh dimension of mastery. | T: This is the seventh dimension of (mental) mastery.
an10.29:13.1 | S: Not perceiving form internally, someone sees forms externally, white, with white color and white appearance. | T: (h) “One percipient of the formless internally sees forms externally as white, white in their color, white in their features, white in their glow.
an10.29:13.2 | S: They’re like the morning star that’s white, with white color and white appearance. Or a cloth from Varanasi that’s smoothed on both sides, white, with white color and white appearance. | T: Just as the morning star is white, white in its color, white in its features, white in its glow, or just as Vārāṇasī muslin, smooth on both sides, is white, white in its color, white in its features, white in its glow,
an10.29:13.3 | S: In the same way, not perceiving form internally, someone sees forms externally, white, with white color and white appearance. | T: in the same way one percipient of the formless internally sees forms externally as white, white in their color, white in their features, white in their glow.

