an10.29:15.1 | S: There are four ways of practice. | T: [6] “There are these four modes of practice.
an10.29:15.2 | S: What four? | T: Which four?
an10.29:15.3 | S: Painful practice with slow insight, | T: Painful practice with slow intuition,
an10.29:15.4 | S: painful practice with swift insight, | T: painful practice with quick intuition,
an10.29:15.5 | S: pleasant practice with slow insight, and | T: pleasant practice with slow intuition,
an10.29:15.6 | S: pleasant practice with swift insight. | T: and pleasant practice with quick intuition.
an10.29:15.7 | S: These are the four ways of practice. | T: These are the four modes of practice.
an10.29:16.1 | S: The best of these four ways of practice is the pleasant practice with swift insight. | T: Now, of these four modes of practice, this is supreme: pleasant practice with quick intuition.
an10.29:16.2 | S: Some sentient beings practice like this. | T: And there are beings whose practice is like this.
an10.29:16.3 | S: But even the sentient beings who practice like this decay and perish. | T: Yet even in the beings whose practice is like this there is still aberration, there is change.
an10.29:16.4 | S: Seeing this, a learned noble disciple grows disillusioned with it. | T: Seeing this, the instructed disciple of the noble ones grows disenchanted with that.
