an10.29:17.1 | S: There are these four perceptions. | T: [7] “There are these four perceptions.
an10.29:17.2 | S: What four? | T: Which four?
an10.29:17.3 | S: One individual perceives the limited. | T: One perceives the limited [ordinary perceptions].
an10.29:17.4 | S: One individual perceives the expansive. | T: One perceives the enlarged [the mind in jhāna].
an10.29:17.5 | S: One individual perceives the limitless. | T: One perceives the immeasurable [the mind in the brahmavihāras].
an10.29:17.6 | S: One individual, aware that ‘there is nothing at all’, perceives the dimension of nothingness. | T: One perceives the dimension of nothingness: ‘There is nothing.’
an10.29:17.7 | S: These are the four perceptions. | T: These are the four perceptions.
an10.29:18.1 | S: The best of these four perceptions is when someone, aware that ‘there is nothing at all’, perceives the dimension of nothingness. | T: Now, of these four perceptions, this is supreme: when one perceives the dimension of nothingness: ‘There is nothing.’
an10.29:18.2 | S: Some sentient beings perceive like this. | T: And there are beings who are percipient in this way.
an10.29:18.3 | S: But even the sentient beings who perceive like this decay and perish. | T: Yet even in the beings who are percipient in this way there is still aberration, there is change.
an10.29:18.4 | S: Seeing this, a learned noble disciple grows disillusioned with it. | T: Seeing this, the instructed disciple of the noble ones grows disenchanted with that.
an10.29:18.5 | S: Their desire fades away even for the foremost, let alone the inferior. | T: Being disenchanted with that, he becomes dispassionate toward what is supreme, and even more so toward what is inferior.
an10.29:19.1 | S: This is the best of the convictions of outsiders, that is: ‘I might not be, and it might not be mine. I will not be, and it will not be mine.’ | T: [8] “The supreme view-point external (to the Dhamma) is this: ‘I should not be; it should not occur to me; I will not be; it will not occur to me.’
an10.29:19.2 | S: When someone has such a view, you can expect | T: Of one with this view it may be expected that
an10.29:19.3 | S: that they will not be attracted to continued existence, | T: ‘the unloathsomeness of becoming will not occur to him,
an10.29:19.4 | S: and they will not be repulsed by the cessation of continued existence. | T: and the loathsomeness of the cessation of becoming will not occur to him.’
an10.29:19.5 | S: Some sentient beings have such a view. | T: And there are beings who have this view.
an10.29:19.6 | S: But even the sentient beings who have views like this decay and perish. | T: Yet even in the beings who have this view there is still aberration, there is change.
an10.29:19.7 | S: Seeing this, a learned noble disciple grows disillusioned with it. | T: Seeing this, the instructed disciple of the noble ones grows disenchanted with that.
an10.29:19.8 | S: Their desire fades away even for the foremost, let alone the inferior. | T: Being disenchanted with that, he becomes dispassionate toward what is supreme, and even more so toward what is inferior.
an10.29:20.1 | S: There are some ascetics and brahmins who advocate the ultimate purity of the spirit. | T: [9] “There are some contemplatives & brahmans who declare the foremost purity of the spirit.
an10.29:20.2 | S: This is the best of the advocates of the ultimate purity of the spirit, that is, when someone, going totally beyond the dimension of nothingness, enters and remains in the dimension of neither perception nor non-perception. | T: Now, of those who proclaim the foremost purity of the spirit, these are supreme: those who, with the complete transcending of the dimension of nothingness, enter & remain in the dimension of neither perception nor non-perception
an10.29:20.3 | S: They teach Dhamma in order to directly know and realize this. | T: and who, having directly known & realized this, teach their Dhamma.
an10.29:20.4 | S: Some sentient beings have such a doctrine. | T: And there are beings who teach in this way.
an10.29:20.5 | S: But even the sentient beings who have such a doctrine decay and perish. | T: Yet even in the beings who teach in this way there is still aberration, there is change.
an10.29:20.6 | S: Seeing this, a learned noble disciple grows disillusioned with it. | T: Seeing this, the instructed disciple of the noble ones grows disenchanted with that.
an10.29:20.7 | S: Their desire fades away even for the foremost, let alone the inferior. | T: Being disenchanted with that, he becomes dispassionate toward what is supreme, and even more so toward what is inferior.
an10.29:21.1 | S: There are some ascetics and brahmins who advocate ultimate extinguishment in this very life. | T: [10] “There are some contemplatives & brahmans who proclaim the foremost unbinding in the here & now.
an10.29:21.2 | S: This is the best of those who advocate extinguishment in this very life, that is, liberation by not grasping after truly understanding the origin, disappearance, gratification, drawback, and escape of the six fields of contact. | T: Now, of those who proclaim the foremost unbinding in the here & now, this is supreme: liberation through non-clinging, having known, as they have come to be, the origination, the passing away, the allure, the drawbacks of, & the escape from the six sense-contact media.
an10.29:21.3 | S: Though I state and assert this, certain ascetics and brahmins misrepresent me with the incorrect, hollow, false, untruthful claim: | T: And when I teach that, when I point that out, some contemplatives & brahmans accuse me of being false, unfactual, hollow, vain, (saying,)
an10.29:21.4 | S: ‘The ascetic Gotama doesn’t advocate the complete understanding of sensual pleasures, forms, or feelings.’ | T: ‘The contemplative Gotama does not declare the full comprehension of sensuality, does not declare the full comprehension of forms, does not declare the full comprehension of feelings.’
an10.29:21.5 | S: But I do advocate the complete understanding of sensual pleasures, forms, and feelings. And I advocate complete extinguishment with no fuel for grasping in this very life, wishless, quenched, and cooled.” | T: But I do declare the full comprehension of sensuality, I do declare the full comprehension of forms, I do declare the full comprehension of feelings. Unhungering, unbound, cooled in the here & now, I declare total unbinding through lack of clinging/sustenance.”
an10.29:21.6 | P: Navamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an6.63
an6.63:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, I will teach you a penetrative exposition of the teaching. | T: “Monks, I will teach you the penetrative explanation that is a Dhamma explanation.
an6.63:1.2 | S: Listen and apply your mind well, I will speak.” | T: Listen & pay close attention. I will speak.”
an6.63:1.3 | S: “Yes, sir,” they replied. | T: “As you say, lord,” the monks responded to the Blessed One.
an6.63:1.4 | S: The Buddha said this: | T: The Blessed One said:
an6.63:2.1 | S: “Mendicants, what is the penetrative exposition of the teaching? | T: “And which penetrative explanation is a Dhamma explanation?
an6.63:2.2 | S: Sensual pleasures should be known. And their source, diversity, result, cessation, and the practice that leads to their cessation should be known. | T: “Sensuality should be known. The cause by which sensuality comes into play should be known. The diversity in sensuality should be known. The result of sensuality should be known. The cessation of sensuality should be known. The path of practice for the cessation of sensuality should be known.
an6.63:3.1 | S: Feelings should be known. And their source, diversity, result, cessation, and the practice that leads to their cessation should be known. | T: “Feeling should be known. The cause by which feeling comes into play should be known. The diversity in feeling should be known. The result of feeling should be known. The cessation of feeling should be known. The path of practice for the cessation of feeling should be known.
an6.63:4.1 | S: Perceptions should be known. And their source, diversity, result, cessation, and the practice that leads to their cessation should be known. | T: “Perception should be known. The cause by which perception comes into play should be known. The diversity in perception should be known. The result of perception should be known. The cessation of perception should be known. The path of practice for the cessation of perception should be known.
an6.63:5.1 | S: Defilements should be known. And their source, diversity, result, cessation, and the practice that leads to their cessation should be known. | T: “Effluents [āsava] should be known. The cause by which effluents come into play should be known. The diversity in effluents should be known. The result of effluents should be known. The cessation of effluents should be known. The path of practice for the cessation of effluents should be known
an6.63:6.1 | S: Deeds should be known. And their source, diversity, result, cessation, and the practice that leads to their cessation should be known. | T: “Kamma should be known. The cause by which kamma comes into play should be known. The diversity in kamma should be known. The result of kamma should be known. The cessation of kamma should be known. The path of practice for the cessation of kamma should be known.
an6.63:7.1 | S: Suffering should be known. And its source, diversity, result, cessation, and the practice that leads to its cessation should be known. | T: “Stress should be known. The cause by which stress comes into play should be known. The diversity in stress should be known. The result of stress should be known. The cessation of stress should be known. The path of practice for the cessation of stress should be known.
an6.63:8.1 | S: ‘Sensual pleasures should be known. And their source, diversity, result, cessation, and the practice that leads to their cessation should be known.’ That’s what I said, | T: [1] “‘Sensuality should be known. The cause by which sensuality comes into play… The diversity in sensuality… The result of sensuality… The cessation of sensuality… The path of practice for the cessation of sensuality should be known.’ Thus it has been said.
an6.63:8.2 | S: but why did I say it? | T: In reference to what was it said?
an6.63:8.3 | S: There are these five kinds of sensual stimulation. | T: “There are these five strings of sensuality:
an6.63:8.4 | S: Sights known by the eye, which are likable, desirable, agreeable, pleasant, sensual, and arousing. | T: forms cognizable via the eye—agreeable, pleasing, charming, endearing, enticing, linked to sensual desire;
an6.63:8.5 | S: Sounds known by the ear … | T: sounds cognizable via the ear…
an6.63:8.6 | S: Smells known by the nose … | T: aromas cognizable via the nose…
an6.63:8.7 | S: Tastes known by the tongue … | T: flavors cognizable via the tongue…
an6.63:8.8 | S: Touches known by the body, which are likable, desirable, agreeable, pleasant, sensual, and arousing. | T: tactile sensations cognizable via the body—agreeable, pleasing, charming, endearing, enticing, linked to sensual desire.
an6.63:8.9 | S: However, these are not sensual pleasures. In the training of the Noble One they’re called ‘kinds of sensual stimulation’. | T: But these are not sensuality. They are called strings of sensuality in the discipline of the noble ones.
an6.63:8.10 | S: Greedy intention is a person’s sensual pleasure. | T: The passion for his resolves is a man’s sensuality,
an6.63:8.11 | S: The world’s pretty things aren’t sensual pleasures. | T: not the beautiful sensual pleasures found in the world.
an6.63:8.12 | S: Greedy intention is a person’s sensual pleasure. | T: The passion for his resolves is a man’s sensuality.
an6.63:8.13 | S: The world’s pretty things stay just as they are, | T: The beauties remain as they are in the world,
an6.63:8.14 | S: but the attentive remove desire for them. | T: while, in this regard, the enlightened subdue their desire.
an6.63:9.1 | S: And what is the source of sensual pleasures? | T: “And what is the cause by which sensuality comes into play?
an6.63:10.2 | S: Contact is their source. | T: Contact is the cause by which sensuality comes into play.
an6.63:11.1 | S: And what is the diversity of sensual pleasures? | T: “And what is the diversity in sensuality?
an6.63:11.2 | S: The sensual desire for sights, sounds, smells, tastes, and touches are all different. | T: Sensuality with regard to forms is one thing, sensuality with regard to sounds is another, sensuality with regard to aromas is another, sensuality with regard to flavors is another, sensuality with regard to tactile sensations is another.
an6.63:11.3 | S: This is called the diversity of sensual pleasures. | T: This is called the diversity in sensuality.
an6.63:12.1 | S: And what is the result of sensual pleasures? | T: “And what is the result of sensuality?
an6.63:12.2 | S: When one who desires sensual pleasures generates a corresponding incarnation, partaking of either good or bad deeds—this is called the result of sensual pleasures. | T: One who wants sensuality produces a corresponding state of existence, on the side of merit or demerit. This is called the result of sensuality.
an6.63:13.1 | S: And what is the cessation of sensual pleasures? | T: “And what is the cessation of sensuality?
an6.63:13.2 | S: When contact ceases, sensual pleasures cease. | T: From the cessation of contact is the cessation of sensuality;
an6.63:13.3 | S: The practice that leads to the cessation of sensual pleasures is simply this noble eightfold path, that is: | T: and just this noble eightfold path—
an6.63:13.4 | S: right view, right purpose, right speech, right action, right livelihood, right effort, right mindfulness, and right immersion. | T: right view, right resolve, right speech, right action, right livelihood, right effort, right mindfulness, right concentration—is the way leading to the cessation of sensuality.
an6.63:14.1 | S: When a noble disciple understands sensual pleasures in this way—and understands their source, diversity, result, cessation, and the practice that leads to their cessation—they understand that this penetrative spiritual life is the cessation of sensual pleasures. | T: “Now when a disciple of the noble ones discerns sensuality in this way, the cause by which sensuality comes into play in this way, the diversity in sensuality in this way, the result of sensuality in this way, the cessation of sensuality in this way, & the path of practice leading to the cessation of sensuality in this way, then he discerns this penetrative holy life as the cessation of sensuality.
an6.63:14.2 | S: ‘Sensual pleasures should be known. And their source, diversity, result, cessation, and the practice that leads to their cessation should be known.’ | T: “‘Sensuality should be known. The cause by which sensuality comes into play… The diversity in sensuality… The result of sensuality… The cessation of sensuality… The path of practice for the cessation of sensuality should be known.’
an6.63:14.3 | S: That’s what I said, and this is why I said it. | T: Thus it has been said, and in reference to this was it said.
an6.63:15.1 | S: ‘Feelings should be known. And their source, diversity, result, cessation, and the practice that leads to their cessation should be known.’ | T: [2] “‘Feeling should be known. The cause by which feeling comes into play… The diversity in feeling… The result of feeling… The cessation of feeling… The path of practice for the cessation of feeling should be known.’
an6.63:15.2 | S: That’s what I said, but why did I say it? | T: Thus it has been said. In reference to what was it said?
an6.63:15.3 | S: There are these three feelings: | T: “There are these three kinds of feeling:
an6.63:15.4 | S: pleasant, painful, and neutral. | T: a feeling of pleasure, a feeling of pain, & feeling of neither pleasure nor pain.
an6.63:16.1 | S: And what is the source of feelings? | T: “And what is the cause by which feeling comes into play?
an6.63:16.2 | S: Contact is their source. | T: Contact is the cause by which feeling comes into play.
an6.63:17.1 | S: And what is the diversity of feelings? | T: “And what is the diversity in feeling?
an6.63:17.2 | S: There are pleasant feelings of the flesh, pleasant feelings not of the flesh, painful feelings of the flesh, painful feelings not of the flesh, neutral feelings of the flesh, and neutral feelings not of the flesh. | T: There is the feeling of pleasure connected with the baits of the world. There is the feeling of pleasure not connected with the baits of the world. There is the feeling of pain connected with the baits of the world. There is the feeling of pain not connected with the baits of the world. There is the feeling of neither pleasure nor pain connected with the baits of the world. There is the feeling of neither pleasure nor pain not connected with the baits of the world.
an6.63:17.3 | S: This is called the disparity of feelings. | T: This is called the diversity in feeling.
an6.63:18.1 | S: And what is the result of feelings? | T: “And what is the result of feeling?
an6.63:18.2 | S: When one who feels generates a corresponding incarnation, partaking of either good or bad deeds— | T: One who feels a feeling produces a corresponding state of existence, on the side of merit or demerit.
an6.63:18.3 | S: this is called the result of feelings. | T: This is called the result of feeling.
an6.63:19.1 | S: And what is the cessation of feelings? | T: “And what is the cessation of feeling?
an6.63:19.2 | S: When contact ceases, feelings cease. | T: From the cessation of contact is the cessation of feeling;
an6.63:19.3 | S: The practice that leads to the cessation of feelings is simply this noble eightfold path, that is: | T: and just this noble eightfold path—
an6.63:19.4 | S: right view, right purpose, right speech, right action, right livelihood, right effort, right mindfulness, and right immersion. | T: right view, right resolve, right speech, right action, right livelihood, right effort, right mindfulness, right concentration—is the way leading to the cessation of feeling.
an6.63:20.1 | S: When a noble disciple understands feelings in this way … they understand that this penetrative spiritual life is the cessation of feelings. | T: “Now when a disciple of the noble ones discerns feeling in this way, the cause by which feeling comes into play in this way, the diversity in feeling in this way, the result of feeling in this way, the cessation of feeling in this way, & the path of practice leading to the cessation of feeling in this way, then he discerns this penetrative holy life as the cessation of feeling.

